<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ded Moroz</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Morozko, Father Frost, Grandfather Frost, Old Man Frost</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ded Moroz, whose name is traditionally translated as Father Frost, is a legendary figure of East Slavic tradition similar to Father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-claus">Santa Claus</a>. His origins predate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> as a Slavic personification of winter, and since the 19th century his legend has been shaped by literature, among it Aleksandr Ostrovsky's play The Snow Maiden. He is depicted with a long white beard, a heel-length fur coat and a magic stick, bringing presents at New Year with his granddaughter Snegurochka. In 1928 he was declared an ally of the priest and kulak, but traditions were revived after a letter published in Pravda in 1935, and he became the main symbol of the Soviet New Year's holiday.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ded-moroz.jpg" alt="Ded Moroz" width="309" height="500" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ded_Moroz_Snegurochka_Christmas_card.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its gift-bringer was <b>Ded Moroz</b>, Father Frost: declared in 1928 an ally of the priest and the kulak, he was revived after a letter published in Pravda in 1935 and became the main symbol of the Soviet New Year's holiday, bringing his presents at New Year.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ded Moroz</b>, Father Frost, is a figure of East Slavic tradition whose origins predate Christianity as a Slavic personification of winter, and whose legend has been shaped by literature since the 19th century.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li></ul></section>
